      <!-- Detailed Review: B.Claw -->
      <div id="bclaw" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:16px;padding:32px;margin-bottom:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:white;font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:16px;">B.Claw, by Bounti</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Start with the thing B.Claw does better than almost anyone in this category: it tells you the price. Bounti publishes a free plan that runs about a week of real use with no credit card, then Start at $99 a month, Light at $219, Medium at $349 and Heavy at $599, all month to month, no setup fee, no annual lock-in, upgrades prorated, top-ups in $5 increments inside a comfort zone that defaults to your plan plus $50. Its own pricing page says it plainly: every plan gets the whole agent, with briefings, triage, CMAs, listing prep, memory and standing orders included, and the only difference is how much it runs in a month. Compare that with BoldTrail, CINC or Sierra Interactive, all of which either hide the price behind a demo form or lock you into twelve months, and Bounti is playing a fairer game than most of its competitors. Bounti also answers the team question honestly instead of dodging it: not yet, B.Claw is a single-agent product today, each agent runs their own subscription, and multi-agent, shared inbox and brokerage tiers are on the roadmap but not shipping.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Then look at what it actually does, because it is substantial. B.Claw connects to Gmail and Google Calendar through OAuth rather than by storing passwords, then works across 250 or more tools and, in Bounti's own words, sits on top of whatever CRM you already pay for. Mornings start with a briefing that lands on your phone, with the calendar, the urgent mail and the pipeline already sorted. Every client, lead, lender and attorney reply is drafted in your voice and left in Gmail for one-tap approval, and Bounti is explicit that nothing sends unless you told a job to, so the send button stays yours. CMAs are pulled from your MLS comps using NAR PSA methodology and generated as branded PDFs. Listings get descriptions, virtual staging and video, which is where Bounti started. Standing orders are listed with what they do and when they fire, and you can pause any of them. It is a real product with a real product idea behind it, and the agents who like it like it a lot.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Now the limits, and the honest ones are three. First, and this is the one that matters most, B.Claw does not answer a phone call and it does not hold a WhatsApp conversation. Nothing in the product does. Its AI reads, drafts, summarises and prepares, all inside your own tools, and a lead who rings at 9pm or messages on a Sunday still reaches nobody. For an agent whose leads arrive by email, that is fine. For an agent whose leads call, it means the fastest part of the funnel is still unanswered. Second, B.Claw is a layer you operate, not an operator that replaces you. Every reply is a draft you approve, so if you have a busy week and you do not work the queue, the drafts just sit there, and the value you get out of the subscription is proportional to the attention you put in. That is a deliberate safety design rather than a flaw, but it is a real difference in what you are buying: we build something that sends the follow-up without asking you, and B.Claw builds something that writes it and waits. Third, the subscription runs forever. On the plans an agent actually runs daily, Light is $2,628 a year and Medium is $4,188 a year, year after year, and Heavy is $7,188, on top of whatever CRM, IDX platform and MLS access you are already paying for. Medium for three years is about $12,564 and Heavy is about $21,564, and cancelling takes the agent with it.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          One more honest note, on reviews. There is no independent review base for the real estate product yet. Bounti's G2 page shows 4.9 out of 5 from 7 reviews, but those written reviews are from 2024 and they describe a general B2B prospecting and messaging tool, not today's B.Claw real estate workflow, and Capterra, TrustRadius and GetApp all show zero written user reviews for the real estate listing. A third party review profile checked on 12 November 2026 put it well: the small G2 base describes a 2024 general B2B prospecting product and is not clear evidence for today's B.Claw real-estate workflows. That is not a criticism of the product, it is a statement about how young it is, and it cuts both ways: it means you are early, which is why the free trial matters so much. Take it, connect your Gmail, and see for yourself rather than trusting a page like this one.
        </p>
        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px;margin-bottom:16px;">
          <div>
            <p style="color:white;font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:8px;">Pros</p>
            <ul style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;padding-left:20px;">
              <li>Every price, every limit published, no demo required</li>
              <li>Free trial of about a week with no credit card</li>
              <li>Month to month, no annual lock-in, no setup fee</li>
              <li>250+ integrations, rides on the stack you already pay for</li>
              <li>Email replies drafted in your voice for one-tap approval</li>
              <li>CMAs from your MLS comps, branded, in minutes</li>
              <li>Morning briefing with calendar and pipeline on your phone</li>
              <li>Virtual staging and listing video from the same platform</li>
              <li>Draft-before-send safety and readable, correctable memory</li>
